{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2133 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0f38b0d2e68ae547870b9da2779e27de07a4163fae4b0c06efdfac43b313a9d1" :edb/db-before-t 2132 :edb/db-after-t 2133 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535445 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:12:50.329Z" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119554 1100 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535445 false] [17592186119554 1100 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/5971723fe2da8b35c0c965b25bf084080b2602b52a1099b6c97f637ca62fb2c2.png)" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119554 1145 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nSince $A\\sim B$, the equation\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4\n$$\n\nwhere $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} = x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}, x_{4} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186119554 1145 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/7c7c6711d4453e0e8365dbec263cf23e0c8e4bf05ff091ecc3bf9593797ff538.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119555 1100 "Given that  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/412e5084f88d0eb270b0e8a1f5613f85e1cec2c9f89879ccda30ff4c0794fb3d.png) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119555 1100 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186119555 1145 "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/7bba2b6227c4fe42901e654d6025c54d7937c13743dfc74586fc72c767c31c06.png)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/60eca2d19c167355d263c471cb876e181db49ab5ac65b27561e6fb95bcc21ec0.png)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119555 1145 "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-x_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186119556 1100 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/e85d4892dc0bad0c6019b0434eff3d140fcf654171f6fda480b109b324e7b443.png)" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119556 1100 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535445 false] [17592186119556 1145 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; x_{3} - 4x_{4} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}$. This implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186119556 1145 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/367b1779ba31df69791a24ab485f1715cedb46f57e383053fd17a189c465d09a.png)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/9b2704bbd684106b7c1c7dbe52eb957e65e6634d9adb6ba7fb415b0097af7e64.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119557 1100 "Given the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/13a1fbdcd6620ab5ccc41283da5ded8996053f9a59fd8c820ea786837ae05222.png) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119557 1100 "Given the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186119557 1145 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0} \\}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}\n$$" 13194139535445 false] [17592186119557 1145 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So $\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},$ which means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}.$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186119740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349412 13194139535445 true] [17592186119740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349419 13194139535445 true] [17592186119740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349453 13194139535445 true] [17592186119740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349506 13194139535445 true] [17592186119741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349391 13194139535445 true] [17592186119741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349478 13194139535445 true] [17592186119741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349485 13194139535445 true] [17592186119742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349398 13194139535445 true] [17592186119742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349405 13194139535445 true] [17592186119742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349492 13194139535445 true] [17592186119742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349499 13194139535445 true] [17592186119743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349426 13194139535445 true] [17592186119743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349433 13194139535445 true] [17592186119743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349440 13194139535445 true] [17592186119743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349513 13194139535445 true] [17592186119743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349520 13194139535445 true] [17592186120276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186118131 13194139535445 false] [17592186120276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349447 13194139535445 true] [17592186120276 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535445 true] [17592186120276 1100 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $Null(A)$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535445 false] [17592186120276 1100 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186120276 1145 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186120314 1017 #edb/ref 17592186118359 13194139535445 false] [17592186120314 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349460 13194139535445 true] [17592186120314 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535445 true] [17592186120314 1100 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535445 false] [17592186120314 1100 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535445 true] [17592186120314 1145 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}-3{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}={x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3}=3{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ are basic variables corresponding to t